{
 "id": "2024-zhai-small",
 "name": "Small Urban Open Spaces: PRS-11 Rating",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: twelve virtual scenes of a small urban open space enclosed on three sides by trees and shrubs, with buildings behind, rendered by the authors in Mars and Lumion from a 1.6 m eye height: three site types (plaza, lawn, water) at four sizes (10 x 10 m, 20 x 20 m, 30 x 30 m, 40 x 40 m). As in the paper's between-subject design, each participant sees one scene drawn at random and answers, with the scene on screen, the paper's questions on preference, openness and beauty and the 11 items of the Perceived Restorativeness Scale PRS-11 (fascination, being away, coherence, scope), all on its 11-point scale from 0 = not at all to 10 = very much, then its demographic questions (gender, age, occupation, design background, place grown up, monthly household income) and its attention check asking in which month they take part. Rebuilt from Zhai, Fan, Yu, Gong & Yin (2024), Effects of Spatial Type and Scale of Small Urban Open Spaces on Perceived Restoration: An Online Survey-Based Experiment, Land 13(9): 1370, with the images from its Figure 1 and the questions from Section 2.2 and Table 2 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: plaza_10 ... water_40 = site type and side length in metres; answers are in scene (item codes preference, openness, beauty) and prs (item codes fa1-fa3, ba1-ba3, co1-co3, sc1-sc2 in the order of Table 2), both keyed by image id; each image's type and size and the paper's number of respondents are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: remove respondents who give the wrong month or finish in under 60 seconds; score each PRS dimension and overall restoration as item means; compare site types and sizes with regression models including the demographic variables, and test interactions of site type with age group and with high versus low preference. The paper (1,575 people recruited through WeChat in 2022-2023, 1,130 valid, 81-116 per scene) found that lawns had the highest restorative effects, that site size made no significant difference, so even a 10 x 10 m site was restorative, that people with high preference gained more restoration from lawns than from plazas, and that adults over 50 rated restoration higher while 18- to 25-year-olds showed a larger gain from plaza to natural scenes. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper ran on the Chinese platform Wenjuanxing (Star Questionnaire) and does not state the language of its questions; here the survey is in English and anyone can take part; (2) the instruction text, the order of the questions and the wording of the demographic questions and their answer options are ours, with the paper's categories (age groups 18-25, 26-50, over 50 are formed from the age given; income bands in RMB as in the paper); the paper's regional split (south and north China) is not asked; (3) the gender question adds an 'other or prefer not to say' option; (4) the scene is drawn at random by the page, as by the paper's platform; (5) the images are the figure copies (about 290 x 165 px), shown enlarged.",
 "author": "Zhai, Fan, Yu, Gong & Yin",
