core each PRS dimension and overall restoration as item means; compare site types and sizes with regression models including the demographic variables, and test interactions of site type with age group and with high versus low preference. The paper (1,575 people recruited through WeChat in 2022-2023, 1,130 valid, 81-116 per scene) found that lawns had the highest restorative effects, that site size made no significant difference, so even a 10 x 10 m site was restorative, that people with high preference gained more restoration from lawns than from plazas, and that adults over 50 rated restoration higher while 18- to 25-year-olds showed a larger gain from plaza to natural scenes. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper ran on the Chinese platform Wenjuanxing (Star Questionnaire) and does not state the language of its questions; here the survey is in English and anyone can take part; (2) the instruction text, the order of the questions and the wording of the demographic questions and their answer options are ours, with the paper's categories (age groups 18-25, 26-50, over 50 are formed from the age given; income bands in RMB as in the paper); the paper's regional split (south and north China) is not asked; (3) the gender question adds an 'other or prefer not to say' option; (4) the scene is drawn at random by the page, as by the paper's platform; (5) the images are the figure copies (about 290 x 165 px), shown enlarged.\",\n  \"locale\": \"en\",\n  \"mediaFolders\": [\n   \"scenes\"\n  ],\n  \"pages\": [\n   {\n    \"name\": \"start\",\n    \"elements\": [\n     {\n      \"type\": \"consent\",\n      \"name\": \"consent\",\n      \"title\": \"I agree to take part. Participation is voluntary and my answers are recorded anonymously for research.\",\n      \"isRequired\": true,\n      \"labelTrue\": \"I agree\",\n      \"labelFalse\": \"I do not agree\"\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"name\": \"scene\",\n    \"elements\": [\n     {\n      \"type\": \"matrix